Use atomic variables for ConnectionInterrupted and LBQ currentSize

Carried from upstream https://github.com/moonlight-stream/moonlight-common-c/pull/100
(open as of 2026-08-12). Drop this patch and bump the submodule once it merges.

These are the two variables clang's -fsanitize=thread flags in moonlight-common-c.
Both are plain C objects touched from more than one thread: C volatile orders nothing
and provides no atomicity, so these are formal data races that the compiler is free to
reorder or fold, whatever the underlying ARM load/store happens to do.

Upstream is held up only by MSVC, which still needs /experimental:c11atomics for C11
atomics. That has no bearing on this fork - NDK clang supports stdatomic.h on both ABIs
we ship - so the fix costs us nothing to carry ahead of upstream.

Worth having here rather than waiting, because this fork hits the race routinely instead
of rarely. NvConnection.stop() calls interruptConnection() deliberately outside the
synchronized (MoonBridge.class) block, so it can unblock a startConnection() that is
holding the monitor; that is a concurrent write to ConnectionInterrupted against
moonlight-common-c's own reader threads, by design. Game.stopConnection() reaches it from
three separate entry points, one of which is onStop(), which PiP makes fire on ordinary
backgrounding rather than only on user disconnect. armeabi-v7a is a shipping ABI for us
and is where 32-bit codegen is most likely to make the difference visible.

Deviates from the PR in one place: upstream writes ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(false), which was
deprecated in C17 and removed in C23, and which NDK clang warns about. A plain "= false"
initialiser is equivalent for an object with static storage duration. Re-add the macro
only if upstream's merged version is taken verbatim.

